The {{nihongo|'''Miyoshi Line'''|三好線|Miyoshi-sen}} is a high-capacity rapid transit line in [[Okami]], Okaiken, operated by [[Okami Metro]]. Spanning 52.1 kilometers from the northern suburban peaks of Yuyama to the southern industrial waterfront of Okada, it serves as the critical north–south arterial spine of the metropolitan mass transit network. On maps, diagrams, and signage, the line is officially designated as Line 1 and is identified by the color Lavender and the letter M.

As the oldest and most vital route in the system, the Miyoshi Line handles the highest passenger volume in the region. Its alignment follows the historic Miyoshi-suji Avenue, cutting through the heart of [[Tenkyo-jima]] and the city's premier administrative and commercial districts. It is currently the only line in the Okami Metro network designed to accommodate heavy-duty 10-car formations to manage extreme peak-hour density.

==History==

===Conception and Early Development (1920–1935)===
The origins of the Miyoshi Line are inextricably linked to the Great Okami Modernization Plan of the 1920s. Okami experienced an unprecedented population explosion. The existing surface tram network, primarily the Okami City Tram, was overwhelmed, with streetcars often forced to move at a walking pace through the gridlocked Miyoshi-suji corridor. In 1929, the Municipal High-Speed Railway Bureau was established to oversee the construction of a subterranean alternative. 

[[File:Miyoshi_Construction_1930s.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Construction workers utilizing the pneumatic caisson method near the Garyu River in 1932.]]

Ground was broken in early 1930. The project was immediately recognized as an engineering marvel, as much of the city sat on saturated alluvial soil. Engineers adopted the [[w:Pneumatic caisson|pneumatic caisson]] method—the first major application of the technology in the empire—to sink station boxes and tunnel segments beneath the [[Garyu River]] and the [[Soryu River]]. The inaugural section, running from Okami Central (M20) to Hozumi-Oiwake (M15), opened on June 15, 1935. These "underground palaces" featured high vaulted ceilings, [[w:Art Deco|Art Deco]] lighting, and glazed tilework, serving as symbols of Okami's industrial prowess and civic pride.

===Wartime Resilience and Post-War Recovery (1936–1959)===
During the late stages of the [[Great Adonian War]], the line's deep-level tunnels served as critical air-raid shelters for the population. While surface structures at the northern terminus sustained significant damage from firebombing campaigns conducted by Illypnian coalition forces, the tunnels remained structurally sound due to their depth and reinforced concrete linings. Service resumed within weeks of the 1952 armistice, providing a vital lifeline for the city's reconstruction efforts. In 1951, the Southern Reach to Rokumanji (M30) was completed, providing a necessary link to the southern private railway terminals and facilitating the return of displaced citizens to the southern wards.

===The Adonian Games Push and Expansion (1960–1979)===
The [[Adonian Games]] of 1966 spurred a massive northern push to Tatarabashi, which involved complex engineering to tunnel beneath the heavy rail yards of the Okaiken Railways (OR) mainline. This era marked the transition from the original 6-car platforms to 8-car configurations to support international tourist traffic. The western segment, connecting Yuyama Chuo to Kamiya-cho, was prioritized to shuttle spectators to the newly constructed [[Okami Olympic Stadium]] in the Hozumi district.

However, the latter half of the decade was marked by significant disruption. The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1967–1968 brought construction to a near-standstill as the nation navigated the tumultuous transition from the Imperial system to the [[Federal Republic of Okaiken|Federal Republic]]. The provisional government of [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] froze all major infrastructure spending for three years, delaying the eastern extensions until work resumed in August 1971 under the new republican administration. Material prices had surged during the economic restructuring, and the original contractors had dissolved, forcing the Metro Bureau to renegotiate contracts and redesign the eastern portals to accommodate revised safety standards enacted following the [[1971 Ise Earthquake]].

Throughout the 1970s, the focus shifted to the Technoport Okami project, a massive reclamation effort in the Iyonada bay. The Miyoshi Line was extended toward the coast in phases, reaching Takasago (M37) in 1974. This period saw the introduction of the first stainless-steel rolling stock, replacing the aging wooden-accented cars from the pre-war era.

===The Modern Era and Yuyama Completion (1980–Present)===
The final piece of the northern puzzle, the Yuyama Extension, was plagued by decades of geological instability in the Tatara mountain range. Advanced [[w:Tunnel boring machine|tunnel boring machines]] (TBMs) were eventually utilized to complete the link to Yuyama Chuo (M01) in the late 1990s, finalizing the 42-station route that exists today. In 2015, the Miyoshi Renaissance project was launched to modernize station accessibility and restore historic architectural features ahead of the Okami World Expo 2030.

==Technical Engineering==

===The Miyoshi-Nagashima Deep-Bore Section===
The segment surrounding Miyoshi-Nagashima Station (M22) represents one of the most difficult engineering feats in the history of rapid transit. To reach the island of Nagashima, the line had to navigate a three-way crossing of the Soryu River and several high-density canal systems. Unlike the shallow-cut-and-cover sections used in the northern wards, the Miyoshi-Nagashima underground line was constructed as a double-decked deep-bore tunnel. To avoid disturbing the historical foundations of the Nagashima Merchant Quarter, engineers utilized specialized shield tunneling machines that operated at a depth of 45 meters below sea level. The station itself was built within a massive circular caisson, which now houses the metro platforms at the bottom and a high-speed express bypass track on the level above. This stacked configuration allows for future expansion while maintaining the structural integrity of the surrounding island.

[[File:Miyoshi_Nagashima_CrossSection.png|thumb|right|300px|A cross-section diagram of the stacked tunnel configuration at Miyoshi-Nagashima.]]

===Hydrological Management===
Because the line crosses all five major Dragon Rivers, every station between M14 and M35 is equipped with massive watertight floodgates. These gates, which can be deployed in under 120 seconds, are designed to protect the core Tenkyo-jima tunnels in the event of a catastrophic tsunami or river levee failure. The pump systems at Okami Central are capable of moving 50,000 liters of water per minute, reflecting the extreme engineering requirements of building a 1:1 scale network on an alluvial delta.

==Ridership and Demographics==

The Miyoshi Line serves a catchment area of approximately 16.2 million residents across the Okami Metropolis, deriving its high ridership from the dense residential corridors of Yuyama, Tobe, and Masaki, as well as the commercial heart of the city centered on Okami Central Station. Based on ridership modeling and the 2025 census data, the line captures approximately 9.4% of its catchment population as daily riders—a capture rate consistent with mature rapid transit systems in major metropolitan areas.

